<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>A bigger bet.<br />But how much better?</h2>
  <p>GPT-2’s larger models generally did better on several tasks. How much improvement should you expect before spending more time and computing power?</p>
  <p>A model has <strong>parameters</strong>: the adjustable numbers it learns during training. More parameters, more text, and more computation all cost something. Which one is holding progress back?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>Progress has a shape.</h2>
  <p>In 2020, Jared Kaplan and colleagues measured language models across many sizes and training budgets. They tracked <strong>loss</strong>: a score that penalises a model for assigning low probability to the next piece of real text. Lower is better.</p>
  <p>They found smooth <strong>power laws</strong>: when other resources are sufficient, multiplying one resource by the same factor gives a similar proportional reduction in loss. Each doubling shaves off another fraction.</p>
  <p>A resource can become a bottleneck. A larger model cannot indefinitely make up for too little text. Their fits helped researchers estimate improvements before larger training runs. These were measured patterns, not laws of nature.</p>
