<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>A way to plan the leap.</h2>
  <p>Scaling laws helped researchers estimate how a larger training run might improve text prediction. Kaplan’s analysis favoured larger models stopped early, before their loss had finished improving, over smaller models trained to completion.</p>
  <p>Better next-token scores do not guarantee better reasoning or truthful answers. The next chapter asks what a much larger model can do with a few examples in its prompt.</p>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/gpt-3/"><span>Next · 2020</span><strong>GPT-3 and in-context learning ↗</strong><span>Can examples in a prompt teach a new pattern?</span></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Power laws, compute & limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>What was measured?</h3>
    <p>The main experiments used decoder-only Transformers trained to predict the next token—a piece of text—from earlier tokens, chiefly on WebText2. Loss is average cross-entropy in nats: <code>L = −mean(log P(actual next token | previous tokens))</code>, using natural logarithms. It measures prediction, not the fraction of answers that are correct.</p>
    <h3>A power law, in symbols</h3>
    <p>The separate fits have the form <code>L(X) = (Xc / X)^α</code>. X is the resource, Xc a fitted scale, and α the exponent controlling how quickly loss falls. Table 5 gives α = 0.076 for non-embedding parameter count, 0.095 for dataset tokens, and 0.050 for minimum estimated training compute after adjusting for batch size. A batch is the group of tokens used in one parameter update.</p>
    <p>These are different experiments: the other resources must not be the limiting factor. For example, the parameter fit assumes enough data and training. Doubling parameters in that regime multiplies loss by about 0.95. It does not add five percentage points of accuracy. The fitted scales depend on the dataset and tokenisation.</p>
    <h3>What should the budget buy?</h3>
    <p>The paper estimates training operations as <code>C ≈ 6NBS</code>: N non-embedding parameters, B tokens per batch, S update steps. “Non-embedding” excludes the tables that represent tokens and positions. This approximation omits smaller costs and a context-dependent attention term.</p>
    <p>Table 6 estimates <code>Nopt ∝ Cmin^0.73</code> and, assuming one pass through the data, <code>Dopt ∝ Cmin^0.27</code>. Cmin is estimated minimum compute in the small-batch limit; it is not elapsed time or hardware cost. The finding favours growing the model faster than its training text. It does not say to hold the data fixed forever.</p>
    <h3>A useful pattern has limits</h3>
    <p>The paper explicitly expects its extrapolated trends to break down and does not show that every downstream task improves with loss. In 2022, <a href="/chapters/chinchilla/">Chinchilla</a> revisited the allocation question and found a more even scaling of model size and training tokens. A fitted exponent depends on the experiments behind it.</p>
    <aside class="chapter-companions"><h3>Also in this chapter</h3><p><a href={sourceLink("rae-2021-arxiv-record")}>Gopher (2021)</a> studied a family reaching 280 billion parameters; benefits from scale varied across tasks, with less progress in logical and mathematical reasoning. <a href={sourceLink("chowdhery-2022-arxiv-record")}>PaLM (2022)</a> extended the exploration to 540 billion parameters and reported gains across many language benchmarks.</p></aside>
    <h3>Inside the toy</h3>
    <p>For model units m and text units d, the invented score is <code>1 + m^(−0.3) + d^(−0.7)</code>, with <code>d = budget / m</code>. These constants are chosen for illustration. The curve samples 61 allocations; the slider advances in equal ratios of model size. No training data, real model, paper fit, or actual accuracy measurement is involved.</p>
  </div>
</details>
